{"candidates":[{"claim":"RFC 9110 defines safe methods as those intended only for information retrieval with no server state modification, while idempotent methods are those where repeated invocation yields the same result as a single invocation. Safety is strictly stronger: every safe method is idempotent (a no-op repeated is still a no-op), but not conversely—PUT and DELETE are idempotent yet mutate state. An append-only event log surviving at-least-once delivery requires idempotence of its writers, not safety, because appending is inherently a state mutation: the writer must ensure that re-delivery of the same event produces the same durable log state as single delivery (e.g., via idempotency keys or sequence-number deduplication).","mechanism":"Formally: safety ⟺ f(x) leaves server state unchanged (f is a read-only no-op on state). Idempotence ⟺ f^n(x) = f(x) for all n ≥ 1. Safety trivially implies idempotence since a no-op composed with itself is a no-op. The converse fails: f(x) = set x to constant c is idempotent but not safe. An append-only log's writer performs a mutation (append), so it cannot be safe. To survive at-least-once delivery, the writer must be idempotent: re-appending the same logical event must not produce duplicate durable entries, achieved by deterministic event identity (e.g., content hash, producer+sequence-number) and dedup on replay.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110 does not in fact state that safe methods are idempotent, the implication chain breaks.","If an append-only log can tolerate duplicate entries without violating deterministic replay semantics (e.g., consumers dedup downstream), then strict writer idempotence may not be required.","If the problem's 'safe' refers to a different standard (e.g., crash-safety) rather than RFC 9110's definition, the distinction may shift."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006","SRC_007","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.95},{"claim":"An append-only event log does not require either RFC-safety or RFC-idempotence of its writers in the strict HTTP-method sense; it requires a weaker, domain-specific property: deterministic replay invariance, meaning that re-delivery of the same operation under at-least-once semantics yields a final log state indistinguishable from exactly-once delivery. This is idempotence only in the loose sense, not the RFC 9110 method-level definition, because the log itself defines what 'same operation' means (via event identity / dedup keys), not the HTTP method semantics.","mechanism":"RFC 9110's definitions of safe and idempotent are scoped to HTTP methods and server-visible state. An append-only log's 'operation' is not an HTTP method invocation; it is a logical append. The log requires that the append primitive, combined with a deduplication layer (idempotency key, producer epoch + sequence, or content-addressed storage), is idempotent at the application semantic level. This is a replay-determinism property, not the method-level property RFC 9110 names. The distinction matters because conflating them leads to incorrect assumptions about what guarantees the log provides.","counterconditions":["If the problem explicitly asks within the RFC 9110 definitional framework and expects the answer to map directly to 'idempotent,' this candidate overcomplicates.","If one can show that deterministic replay invariance is extensionally identical to idempotence for all practical purposes in an append-only log, the distinction collapses."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_005","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"Safety and idempotence collapse into the same requirement for append-only log writers when replay mutates durable state: because the append is a mutation, the writer cannot be safe; and because at-least-once delivery can replay the append, the writer must be idempotent. But the deeper insight is that the log's append operation, viewed as a function on the log's logical content, is effectively a no-op on replay (the event is already there), so the writer achieves a quasi-safe property at the semantic level—durable state is unchanged by replay—even though the raw HTTP-level operation is not safe.","mechanism":"At the transport level, append is a mutation (not safe). At the semantic level, if dedup succeeds, replayed append becomes a logical no-op (safe). Thus the system engineer must build idempotence at the application layer to achieve safety at the semantic layer. The two properties are duals across abstraction levels: idempotence of the writer operation is the mechanism by which safety of the logical log state is achieved under replay.","counterconditions":["If dedup fails (race, lost idempotency key), the quasi-safety breaks and duplicate entries corrupt the log.","If the log intentionally allows duplicate physical entries (relying on consumers to dedup), the semantic-safety argument doesn't hold at the log layer."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_008","SRC_006","SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65},{"claim":"The distinction is about intended vs observed semantics: RFC 9110 'safe' means the method is *intended* not to cause server-side effects (a design-time contract), while 'idempotent' means the *observable result* of n invocations equals one invocation (a runtime property). An append-only log requires the runtime property (idempotence) of its writers, not the design-time contract (safety), because the append method is explicitly intended to cause an effect—the log's correctness depends on the effect being the same on replay, not on there being no effect at all.","mechanism":"Safety is a design contract (method author declares intent: no side effects). Idempotence is a behavioral property (observable equivalence under repetition). These are different kinds of statements. The log needs the behavioral guarantee; it does not need—and cannot have—the design contract since append is meant to change state. This resolves the apparent tension: safe ⊂ idempotent because design-time no-effect trivially satisfies behavioral equivalence, but the log only needs the behavioral half.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110 defines both as behavioral rather than one as intentional, this framing is wrong.","If the problem demands only the formal algebraic distinction, the intentional/behavioral framing may be seen as extraneous."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.7},{"claim":"An append-only event log requires neither safety nor idempotence of individual writers; it requires commutativity of concurrent appends and idempotence of the deduplication layer. Writers can freely produce duplicate entries; the log's ordering and dedup infrastructure absorbs the redundancy. The at-least-once delivery guarantee is satisfied by the delivery mechanism, not by the writer being idempotent.","mechanism":"In distributed log architectures (e.g., Kafka, event-sourcing systems), producers send events and the broker may duplicate them under at-least-once. The log tolerates this by assigning monotonically increasing sequence numbers per producer epoch; consumers or a compaction step dedup. The writer's HTTP-level idempotence is irrelevant; the system invariant is maintained by the log's own sequencing and dedup. Thus the required property is systemic, not per-writer.","counterconditions":["If the problem specifically asks about writer-level properties (as RFC 9110 scopes them), this systemic answer may not directly address the question.","If dedup is not feasible (unbounded producer identities, no global sequencing), the log cannot survive at-least-once delivery and this claim fails."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.5},{"claim":"RFC 9110 defines safe and idempotent as orthogonal in principle, with overlap only because the empty set of side effects happens to be idempotent. The append-only log requires idempotence precisely, and the safe property is a red herring: safety forbids the very operation (state mutation) the log exists to perform. The precise distinction is that safety constrains the effect itself (must be null), while idempotence constrains the effect under repetition (must be constant). The log needs constant-on-repetition, not null-effect.","mechanism":"Safety: the set of state transitions caused by f is empty. Idempotence: the set of state transitions caused by f∘f equals the set caused by f. For an append-only log, f's transition set is non-empty (it adds an event), so safety is violated. But f∘f's transition set can equal f's if the second invocation is a dedup no-op—achieving idempotence. The log requires this: constant-on-repetition, explicitly not null-effect.","counterconditions":["If RFC 9110's definitions are not set-theoretic in this way and the formalization is an imposed overlay, it may not match the standard's actual language.","If one can construct an append-only log where repeated appends produce different but replay-deterministic states (e.g., timestamps preserved), the 'constant effect' requirement may be too strong."],"evidence_refs":[],"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006","SRC_007"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.85}],"research_proposals":[{"request_identifier":"rfc9110-idempotent-safe","purpose":"Fetch RFC 9110 sections defining 'safe' and 'idempotent' methods to obtain precise verbatim definitions for evidentiary grounding.","urls":["https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110"]},{"request_identifier":"wiki-idempotence","purpose":"Fetch Wikipedia article on idempotence for the mathematical definition and its application in computer science, to corroborate the formal distinction between safety and idempotence.","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Idempotence"]}]}